   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.4. Increased capacity of Member States to promote peaceful, stable and resilient societies through decent work</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">After the multiple earthquakes that hit Syria on 6 February 2023, causing extensive damage across northern part of Syria, and affecting 8.8 million people,  the Governorate of Aleppo, one of the most severely affected governorates, has achieved the following earthquake responses with support of the ILO:
- The recovery needs of households and workers were assessed and documented; through social dialogue with all the constituents, infrastructure and livelihood recovery plans were developed
- 40 members of the Syrian Order of Architects and Engineers (SOAE) syndicate, responsible for damage assessment, demolition and rehabilitation works, were trained on employment-intensive recovery approaches, building damage assessment methodology, and occupational safety and health practices 
- Infrastructure recovery works were implemented, including debris removal, water sewage repair, sidewalk rehabilitation, and school rehabilitation in the affected community neighbourhoods. In February 2023, the ILO re-established its residence presence in Syria. The ILO contributed to the Syria Earthquake Recovery Needs Assessment (SERNA) in the Livelihoods, Employment and SMEs Sector to estimated the impacts and needs. The ILO has then mobilized its internal reserves to quikly respond to the emrgency to support the affected communities in the Governorate of Aleppo to create immediate decent jobs opportunities with the employment-intensive approach.
The approaches entailed, in addition to 500 decent jobs with a vocational training package created for the affected community members for basic services recovery works, provision of personal protective equipment (PPEs) and damage assessment tools to the SOAE syndicate, provision of grants to the Chamber of Industry and General Federation of Trade Unions to help support affected businesses and workers.</narrative>
   </description>
